1
00:00:02,063 --> 00:00:18,681
In this video we install TransactHub on a laptop, sign in, and visit every screen of every console: the designer studio, the service console, the operations console, and the test bench. Everything here is synthetic: no real customer, no real account.

2
00:00:20,322 --> 00:00:30,860
The laptop stack runs the studio and one bank's runtime as separate services, in Docker containers. One runtime serves one bank. The studio serves every bank.

3
00:00:32,482 --> 00:00:52,322
First, build the three images: the studio, the runtime, and Temporal on SQL Server. The build script runs from the repository root. With no options it builds from source inside Docker. With dash dash local, it uses this machine's dot NET, Node and Go, which is what we did here. These are its last lines.

4
00:00:53,923 --> 00:01:08,280
Next, the secrets. Each person sets their own passwords and client secrets in their shell, never in a file in the repository, and no image holds one. Here are the names this stack needs. We show the names only, never the values.

5
00:01:09,897 --> 00:01:29,737
Four are database passwords: the administrator, the runtime's login, the test bench's and Temporal's. One is the password of the synthetic users. Three are for machines: the release pipeline, the front door, and the engine host's token. Keys are not set here: a one-off container makes them into a volume on first start.

6
00:01:31,345 --> 00:01:50,118
Now check the compose file, and start the stack. Up, dash d, dash dash wait, returns when every service is healthy. The first start takes a few minutes: SQL Server starts, the migrations run once as the database owner, and the runtime's login is granted read and insert only.

7
00:01:51,727 --> 00:02:18,436
Here is what runs. The studio on port 4317. The bank's development sign-in on 8480. The release API, the service console, the operations console, the test bench and the File API on 8481 to 8485. Behind them, the engine host, the native host, Temporal and SQL Server. Every port is on this machine only.

8
00:02:20,059 --> 00:02:30,469
Let us open the studio. On a laptop it uses its own development sign-in, with synthetic users. At a bank, the sign-in is the bank's identity provider.

9
00:02:32,072 --> 00:02:42,546
Each user has roles, and the roles decide which surfaces the studio shows. We sign in as a workflow designer, who builds shared content: the platform and the schemes.

10
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:57,824
The workflow designer: the YAML on the left and the canvas on the right edit the same workflow. The artefact editors hold every other kind: activities, rules, rule sets, tables, maps, ports and simulators.

11
00:02:59,431 --> 00:03:11,356
The catalogue lists every published artefact by its name, its scope and its version. ISO data holds the canonical model, the external code sets, and each scheme's dispatch table.

12
00:03:12,967 --> 00:03:30,183
The AI assistant helps beside the editors, behind the same checks as a person. Import reads an extraction bundle from an older system. The test bench runs any block or workflow against simulators. And the conformance kit runs the scenarios every engine must pass.

13
00:03:31,789 --> 00:03:42,861
Export shows the content branch: the one GitHub branch that is the studio's only store. Governance holds the change sets waiting for approval, the versions, and the audit log.

14
00:03:44,479 --> 00:03:57,876
A bank's configurator sees something else. The bank workspace is where a bank sets its dials, binds its hooks, and enables the schemes it uses. The tenant page shows the bank's users and its own content.

15
00:03:59,479 --> 00:04:08,673
The bank approver approves what the configurator made. A checker is never the maker. The approver lands on governance, where the bank's change sets wait.

16
00:04:10,281 --> 00:04:24,659
Other roles see less. A platform approver approves shared content. An auditor reads the catalogue, governance and the kit. An administrator manages the studio's users, and another administrator approves each change of users.

17
00:04:26,262 --> 00:04:41,963
Now the runtime. Its web app has three consoles: the service console, the operations console, and the test bench. They share one sign-in at the bank's identity provider. On a laptop, that is a development provider with five synthetic users.

18
00:04:43,570 --> 00:04:58,012
A service engineer, a bank approver, an operator, a supervisor and a tester. The runtime keeps no users of its own. A role map turns the provider's groups into the runtime's roles. We sign in as the service engineer.

19
00:04:59,619 --> 00:05:13,251
The service console is for the bank's service engineers. Health shows each component: the store, the read model, the package source, the signing keys, the engine, the disk, and each port the bank has bound, with a short history.

20
00:05:14,855 --> 00:05:25,884
On a laptop some rows are red, and that is expected. The bank's core banking and screening hosts are placeholders until the bank names them. Each failing row links to its runbook.

21
00:05:27,486 --> 00:05:44,488
Content lists the signed versions offered on GitHub: the bank's distributions and its configuration releases. Each can be verified, compared with the active one, and installed. Below are the installed versions, their state, and how many open payments each one holds.

22
00:05:46,095 --> 00:05:57,615
Installing, activating, draining, rolling back and removing each ask for a reason, and each waits for a bank approver, another person, to consent. We will do all of that in part 3.

23
00:05:59,222 --> 00:06:13,238
Logs is the error and event log, filtered by code, leg, scheme, payment or trace. Errors and runbooks is the error dictionary. Each code has its likely causes, its checks, its safe actions and who to escalate to.

24
00:06:14,853 --> 00:06:28,741
Actions holds the troubleshooting actions: retry a step, requeue a dead letter, resend a file, re-verify a package, and more. Each is recorded with its reason. Those that change the system wait for the bank approver.

25
00:06:30,346 --> 00:06:44,938
Backup makes a signed backup of the bank's configuration, and restores one with consent. The assistant answers a service engineer's questions from the runbooks. It is off unless the bank switches it on, and it never changes anything.

26
00:06:46,547 --> 00:06:58,152
The operations console is where the bank's operators see and act on running payments. We sign in as a supervisor. The badge at the top says whether the read model has caught up with the store.

27
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:16,688
Payments lists every payment and bulk file, with filters: scheme, leg, status, hold, dates, amounts, and the fields each scheme's view declares. A filter can be saved. The console has no code for any scheme: each scheme declares its view.

28
00:07:18,292 --> 00:07:33,524
A payment's page shows its fields, its legs, its lines, its timeline of facts, the files kept with it, and its holds and actions. Personal fields are masked. To see one, an operator asks with a reason, and a bank approver must consent.

29
00:07:35,345 --> 00:07:50,726
The work queue holds what waits for a person: holds, parked steps and repairs. Items can be taken, given to someone, escalated and released, one by one or in bulk. Approvals lists the business decisions that wait for a second approver.

30
00:07:52,329 --> 00:08:02,761
Reports gives totals by scheme and status, a summary to group as you like, and the fixed reports each view declares, as CSV. Every download is recorded.

31
00:08:04,363 --> 00:08:18,165
Breaks is the reconciliation queue: where a scheme's report file disagrees with the store. A resolution that changes the system needs the bank approver's consent. Turnaround lists the payments that took longer than their view allows.

32
00:08:19,768 --> 00:08:34,530
Retention lists what is due for erasure, and the legal holds that stop it. Consents is where a bank approver sees the changes others asked for: an unmask, an erasure, the release of a legal hold, a rebuild of the read model.

33
00:08:36,133 --> 00:08:56,463
Registers hold the bank's customer data, starting with the corporate registration. It never goes to the studio or to GitHub. A register maker proposes a change, and a bank approver consents. On this laptop the register is empty, and none of the five users is a register maker, so no change can be proposed here.

34
00:08:58,065 --> 00:09:11,270
The audit trail of the console's own actions has no screen yet. An operator reads it from the console's API. A payment's own audit is its timeline, which we saw. We have recorded the missing screen as an issue.

35
00:09:12,874 --> 00:09:25,610
Last, the runtime's test bench, for the bank's testers. It runs a payment on the real engines, against the interpreter as a reference, on SQL Server or PostgreSQL. We sign in as the tester.

36
00:09:27,212 --> 00:09:39,073
Run: choose a profile, the engines, the database, the bank and the simulators, then the data. Temporal is the default engine. DTFx, Elsa and Flowable can be started for a run.

37
00:09:40,687 --> 00:09:51,396
Test data is the library of synthetic sample files, each with what it is expected to do. Suite holds the regression cases, and history the runs that have been made.

38
00:09:52,998 --> 00:10:04,518
That is every screen of the laptop stack. In part 2 we look at the architecture behind them. In part 3 we follow one payment end to end, from the studio to the operations console.
